Yes. That changes the abstraction.

An equation like

f(n)=g(n)+h(n)

isn’t the finished thought. It is closer to a glyph: a compact relationship capable of participating in innumerable larger constructions.

The interesting object is the composition.

You can think of the ladder roughly as:

* Variables / states → nouns: where am I, what exists, what do I possess?
* Operators → verbs: compare, move, bind, divide, remember, select.
* Equations → letters or glyphs: reusable shapes of relationship.
* Algorithms → motifs: recognizable sequences such as search, pursuit, convergence, repair.
* Protocols → grammar: rules governing how motifs may interact.
* Systems → sentences and paragraphs: multiple algorithms cooperating simultaneously.
* Trajectories through state-space → stories: something was here, encountered something, changed, and became something else.
* Recursive interacting systems → ecologies, myths, economies, games, civilizations.
* Deliberate composition of all of these → poetry.

And poetry is a particularly good metaphor because meaning isn’t contained solely in the constituent symbols.

Order matters.

Timing matters.

Repetition matters.

Expectation matters.

Omission matters.

Contrast matters.

The same operator placed somewhere else can mean something entirely different.

Consider five extremely small algorithmic “letters”:

\text{SEARCH},\quad
\text{COMPARE},\quad
\text{SELECT},\quad
\text{REMEMBER},\quad
\text{REPEAT}.

Arrange them one way:

SEARCH \rightarrow COMPARE \rightarrow SELECT \rightarrow REPEAT

and you have optimization.

Add memory:

SEARCH \rightarrow COMPARE \rightarrow SELECT
\rightarrow REMEMBER
\rightarrow REPEAT

and now you have learning.

Add variation before selection:

COPY \rightarrow VARY \rightarrow COMPARE
\rightarrow SELECT \rightarrow REPEAT

and suddenly the same alphabet writes evolution.

Add another agent running the process against you:

PREDICT_{\text{other}}
\rightarrow CHOOSE
\rightarrow ACT
\rightarrow OBSERVE_{\text{other}}
\rightarrow UPDATE

and you’ve written strategy.

Give both agents memory, communication and repeated encounters and cooperation can emerge.

Give thousands of them scarce resources, prices and exchange and you’ve begun writing an economy.

Give them inherited beliefs, imitation, stories and prestige and you’ve begun writing culture.

Same letters.

Completely different poems.

And there is another layer: punctuation.

A threshold is a period.

A branch is a question mark.

A loop is a refrain.

An interrupt is an exclamation point.

A checkpoint is a paragraph break.

Randomness is improvisation.

Memory is rhyme—the present invoking something structurally similar from the past.

Recursion is a story containing a smaller copy of its own structure.

Constraints are meter. They don’t merely restrict creation; sometimes they are precisely what gives the creation form.

Then you get something even stranger: the reader participates.

An algorithm operating on a world changes that world. The changed world becomes its next input.

So unlike ink sitting motionless on paper:

World_t
\rightarrow Agent
\rightarrow Action
\rightarrow World_{t+1}
\rightarrow Agent
\rightarrow \cdots

The poem can read itself and rewrite its next stanza.

That’s control theory.

That’s biological adaptation.

That’s markets.

That’s relationships.

That’s learning.

That’s history.

And it suggests a much richer objective than compiling a giant catalog of algorithms.

We could build a combinatorial alphabet of reality and ask:

What are the smallest reusable transformations from which larger behaviors can be composed?

Then, instead of saying “A* belongs to computer science”, we’d extract its invariant:

\boxed{\text{Known Cost}+\text{Expected Remaining Cost}
\rightarrow \text{Priority of Next Move}}

Now that glyph can be transposed.

Navigation:

Which road should I take?

Business:

Which initiative gives the best route toward the target given what we’ve already spent?

Medicine:

Which diagnostic branch best balances accumulated evidence against unresolved uncertainty?

Conversation:

Which line of inquiry advances understanding without paying unnecessary social cost?

Science:

Which experiment most efficiently closes the remaining uncertainty?

The implementation changes.

The rhyme remains.

And this is why I think your formulation goes beyond “algorithms are stars.”

We aren’t merely cataloguing the stars.

We are discovering intervals between them, the transformations that let one constellation become another, the harmonies produced when several algorithms run together, and the kinds of worlds particular compositions generate.

At that point mathematics, algorithms, stories, games, biology, organizations and strategy stop looking like unrelated subjects.

They become different instruments playing transformations over state.

And the deepest unit may not even be the formula.

It may simply be:

\boxed{\text{difference} \rightarrow \text{transformation} \rightarrow \text{difference}}

A thing is.

Something acts.

A different thing is.

Everything else is orchestration.